==English==

===Alternative forms===
* [[theosophie]] {{qualifier|archaic}}

===Etymology===
{{post|1640}} {{sense|implied in [[theosophical]]}} "knowledge of divine things obtained through mystic study;"<ref name="OEtD"/>
from {{etyl|ML.|en}} {{ante|880}} {{term|theosophia||||lang=la|lit=|pos=}},
from {{etyl|grc|en}} {{circa|500}} {{term|θεόσοφία||wisdom concerning God or things divine|tr=theosofía|lang=grc|lit=|pos=n}},
from {{term|θεόσοφος||one wise about God|tr=theósofós|lang=grc|lit=|pos=a}},
from {{term|θεός||god|tr=theós|lang=grc|lit=|pos=n}} + {{term|σοφία||skill, knowledge of, acquaintance with; philosophy|tr=sofía|lang=grc|lit=wisdom|pos=n}},
from {{term|σοφός||wise, learned|tr=sofós|lang=grc|lit=|pos=a}};<ref name="OEtD"/><ref name="OED-online"/>
alternatively, {{confix|theo|sophy|lang=en}}.<ref name="Collins"/>

The Greek form {{lang|grc|θεόσοφία}} (''theósophía'') is found in {{w|Greek magical papyri}} and was assimilated by [[Neoplatonism]].<ref name="ODCC"/>
{{w|Porphyry}}, a Pagan Neoplatonist, associated it with {{w|Indian philosophy}}; which suggests that it could be the Greek analog of the [[Sanskrit]] ''{{w|brahmavidya}}''.<ref name="ODCC"/>
It is found, {{circa|485–525}}<ref name="Corrigan&Harrington2015"/> in works by {{w|Pseudo-Dionysius the Areopagite}}, a Christian Neoplatonist, and {{w|Johannes Scotus Eriugena}}, who, {{circa|860}}<ref name="Moran2008"/> translated Pseudo-Dionysius and introduced the term into Latin.<ref name="ODCC"/>
It was revived {{post|1600}} in both Latin and vernacular forms to describe the kind of [[speculation]] which is found in the Jewish {{w|Kabbalah}}.<ref name="ODCC"/>
It is often specifically associated with authors such as {{w|Heinrich Cornelius Agrippa}}, {{w|Paracelsus}}, {{w|Robert Fludd}}, {{w|Jakob Böhme}}, {{w|Emanuel Swedenborg}}, and others.<ref name="OEtD"/><ref name="OED-online"/><ref name="ODCC"/>
 

