===Noun===
{{wikipedia}}
{{en-noun|-|theosophies}}

Applied variously over the years.<ref name="OEtD"/><ref name="OED-online"/>

# {{context|philosophy|religion|lang=en}} Any [[philosophical]] or [[religious]] [[system]] based upon a [[claim]] of [[intuitive]] or [[mystical]] [[insight]] into [[divine]] [[nature]], or a claim of divine [[revelation]],<ref name="Cent1911"/><ref name="Collins"/><ref name="W20th"/> or a claim of [[spiritual]] [[ecstasy]],<ref name="ODE"/> and by that, claim to gain a spiritual insight superior to [[empirical]] knowledge.<ref name="W20th"/> Any [[belief]] system based upon mystical insight into the [[nature]] of God and nature of [[soul]];<ref name="AHD"/><ref name="WordNet3"/> a direct, as distinguished from a revealed, knowledge of God, attained by extraordinary [[intellectual]] or spiritual [[enlightenment]]; especially, a direct insight into the processes of the divine mind, and the interior relations of the divine nature.<ref name="W1913"/> Any philosophical or mystical system which claims to attain communication with God and superior [[spirit]]s, and resultant [[supernatural]] knowledge, by [[physical]] processes, such as [[theurgy]] of some ancient [[Platonist]]s, or [[alchemy]] of German [[fire philosopher]]s.<ref name="W1913"/> It differs from most philosophical systems which start with [[premise]]s about [[phenomena]] then, from those, [[deduce]] [[conclusion]]s concerning God, while it, in an opposite sequence, starts with premises about [[assume]]d knowledge of God then, from those, proceeds to [[explanations]] concerning phenomena.<ref name="Cent1911"/>
#*{{quote-magazine|last=Lee|first=Tanith|authorlink=Tanith Lee|title=Bite-Me-Not, or Fleur de Feu|pages=|magazine={{w|Isaac Asimov's Science Fiction Magazine}}|editor=|year=1984|month=Oct|volume=8|location=|publisher=Davis Publications|issn=0162-2188|url=http://books.google.com/books?id=sNhaAAAAYAAJ|passage=They have their own traditions of art and science. They do not {{...}} discuss God or metaphysics or men. {{...}} All their wisdom and '''theosophy''', and all their grasp of beauty, truth or love, is in the singing. {{...}} They look unloving enough, and so they are. Pitiless fallen angels.}}
#*{{quote-journal|last=Lundberg|first=Axel|title=A system of occidental occultism|page=214|journal=The open court|volume=42|issue=4|year=1928|month=Apr|location=Chicago|publisher={{w|Open Court Publishing Company|Open Court}}|oclc=609694021|url=http://opensiuc.lib.siu.edu/ocj/vol1928/iss4/3|passage=Whatever significance we may attach to the above definitions they seem to indicate that the demarkation line between '''Theosophy''' and Mysticism is very thin and is to be found rather in the difference of methods than of results, which latter in both cases are the same, though in the former they are arrived at by means of spontaneous activity and efforts while in the latter a passive expectance serves the same purpose.}}
#*{{quote-book|editors={{w|Johann Jakob Herzog}}; {{w|Philip Schaff}}; {{w|Samuel Macauley Jackson}}|chapter=Theosophy|title=A religious encyclopaedia: or dictionary of biblical, historical, doctrinal, and practical theology|page=2348|year=1889|location=New York|publisher=Christian Literature|oclc=676862728|pageurl=http://books.google.com/books?id=o08XAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA2348|passage='''Theosophy''' {{...}} is distinguished from mysticism, speculative theology, and other forms of philosophy and theology, to which it bears a certain resemblance, by its claims of direct divine inspiration, immediate divine revelation, and its want, more or less conspicuous, of dialectical exposition. It is found among all nations — Hindus, Persians, Arabs, Greeks (the later Neo-Platonism), and Jews (Cabala) — and presents itself variously under the form of magic ({{w|Heinrich Cornelius Agrippa|Agrippa of Netteshelm}}, {{w|Paracelsus}}),or vision ({{w|Emanuel Swedenborg|Swedenborg}}, {{w|Louis Claude de Saint-Martin|Saint Martin}}), or rapt contemplation ({{w|Jacob Boehme}}, {{w|Konrad Öttinger|Oettinger}}).}}
#*{{quote-book|last=Seth|first=Andrew|authorlink=Andrew Seth Pringle-Pattison|chapter=Theosophy|title={{w|Encyclopaedia Britannica}}|volume=23|page=278|year=1888|location=Edinburgh|publisher=A. and C. Black|oclc=707031870|pageurl=http://books.google.com/books?id=xE01AQAAMAAJ&pg=PA278|passage={{...}} it is characteristic of '''theosophy''' that it starts with an explication of the Divine essence, and endeavours to deduce the phenomenal universe from the play of forces within the Divine nature itself. It is thus differentiated at once from all philosophic systems which attempt to rise from an analysis of phenomena to a knowledge, more or less adequate, of the existence and nature of God.}}
#*{{quote-journal|author=Anonymous|title=Reviewed Work: ''Die Seele indischer und hellenischer Philosophie in den gespenstern moderner Geisterseherei'' by Adolph Bastian|page=546|journal={{w|The American Journal of Psychology}}|volume=1|issue=3|year=1888|month=May|location=|publisher=University of Illinois Press|issn=0002-9556|jstor=1411087|passage=As {{w|Gustav Jäger (naturalist)|Jager}}'s idea of soul as something which is smelled is met with among many savage races (even animals whose sense of smell takes the place of sight in man perhaps believing in olfactory ghosts, Marville claiming to see in a magnifying glass that the exhalations of friends fused and those of enemies mutually repelled each other), so '''theosophy''' is but a recrudescence of a belief widely proclaimed in the twelfth century and held to in some form by many barbaric tribes. [[Spiritism]] and "esoteric Buddhism" illustrate the oldest and most widespread of popular superstitions against which Aristotle so vigorously protested, that the soul is something material, apprehensible to vision, smell, taste, touch, or audition, though finer and perhaps smaller than the body.}}
#*{{quote-book|last=Caird|first=Edward|authorlink=Edward Caird|title=A critical account of the philosophy of Kant : with an historical introduction|page=17|year=1877|location=Glasgow|publisher=J. Maclehose|oclc=630876317|pageurl=http://books.google.com/books?id=0YmSpVpF77UC&pg=PA17|passage=When we try to trace the effect of this experience on the philosophies or '''theosophies''' that close the period, record of Greek speculation, we are somewhat embarrassed by the increasing imperfection of logical method and form, that characterises the transition period {{...}}}}
#*{{quote-book|last=Lewes|first=George H.|authorlink=George Henry Lewes|title=The life and works of Goethe : with sketches of his age and contemporaries from published and unpublished sources|volume=2|pages=393–394|year=1855|location=London|publisher=D. Nutt|oclc=793725016|pageurl=http://books.google.com/books?id=pUM6AAAAcAAJ&pg=PA393|passage=There are two aspects under which religion may be considered: the divine aspect, and the human aspect; in the one it is '''Theosophy''', in the other {{w|Ethics}}. {{w|Johann Wolfgang von Goethe|Goethe}}'s '''Theosophy''' was that of {{w|Baruch Spinoza|Spinoza}}, modified by his own poetical tendencies; it was not a geometrical, but a poetical [[Pantheism]].}}
#*{{quote-journal|last=Bojames|first=|authorlink=|title=Outline is the craniognomic system of Dr. Gall of Vienna|page=235|journal={{w|Journal of Natural Philosophy, Chemistry, and the Arts}}|volume=4|issue=|month=Apr|year=1803|location=London|publisher=W. Stratford|oclc=607535562|pageurl=http://hdl.handle.net/2027/nyp.33433069057366?urlappend=%3Bseq=619|passage=The organ of '''theosophy''' occupies the most elevated part of the ''{{w|os frontis}}''. {{...}} It is excessively developed in religious fanatics, and in men who have become recluse through superstition and religious notions.}}
#*{{quote-journal|author=Anonymous|title=Observations respecting the History of Physiognomy : By Thomas Cooper, Esq.|page=208|journal={{w|The European magazine, and London review}}|year=1792|month=Sep|location=London|publisher={{w|Philological Society of London}}|oclc=560258308|pageurl=http://hdl.handle.net/2027/mdp.39015018405657?urlappend=%3Bseq=234|passage={{...}} the defenders of [[physiognomy]], the professed teachers of the science, have been either teachers and defenders of [[alchemy]], [[magic]], [[astrology]], or '''theosophy''', or all of them; and of course that it was utterly impossible that these doctrines should be rejected without some contamination on the character of the companion science of physiognomy; {{...}}}}
#*{{quote-book|last=Hartley|first=Thomas|authorlink=|title=A discourse of mistakes concerning religion, enthusiasm, experiences, etc.|page=28|year=1759|location=Germantown|publisher=Reprinted by C. Sower|oclc=862904327|pageurl=http://hdl.handle.net/2027/uc1.31175035160780?urlappend=%3Bseq=34|passage=We have our skillful workmen of diﬀerent denominations, that need not be ashamed, some demonstrated in a method of proof not to be gainsaid, that the sacred truths of our religion have their sure foundations in the depths of a divine '''theosophy''' {{...}}}}
#*{{quote-book|last=Cudworth|first=Ralph|authorlink=Ralph Cudworth|title=The true intellectual system of the universe : the first part, wherein, all the reason and philosophy of atheism is confuted; and its impossibility demonstrated|page=377|year=1678|location=London|publisher=Richard Royston|oclc=645150372|pageurl=http://hdl.handle.net/2027/uc2.ark:/13960/t02z1kp7t?urlappend=%3Bseq=395|passage=But {{w|Xenophanes}} his '''theosophy''', or divine philosophy, is most fully declared by {{w|Simplicius of Cilicia|Simplicius}} out of {{w|Theophrastus}} in this manner, {{...}}}}
#*{{quote-book|author=Eugenius Philalethes (pseud. of Thomas Vaughan)|authorlink=Thomas Vaughan (philosopher)|chapter=The author to the reader|title=Anthroposophia theomagica : or A discourse of the nature of man and his state after death|page=B4|year=1650|location=London|publisher=Printed by T. W. for H. Blunden|oclc=606999743|pageurl=http://hdl.handle.net/2027/gri.ark:/13960/t13n2zg24?urlappend=%3Bseq=21|passage=It is not the primitive trueth{{SIC}} of creation, not the ancient, reall{{SIC}} '''[[theosophie]]''' of the Hebrews and Egyptians, but a certain preternaturall{{SIC}} up start, a vomit of Aristotle, which his followers with so much diligence lick up, and swallow.}}
#{{context|often initial letter capitalized|lang=en}} The system of beliefs and doctrines of the {{w|Theosophical Society}} which claims to encompass supernatural knowledge, about the world found in various [[esoteric]] [[tradition]]s contained in the [[exoteric]] doctrines of historical religions particularly [[Brahmanism]] and [[Buddhism]], profounder than the systematic knowledge of the natural world gained through empirical science;<ref name="OED-online"/> especially the belief in reincarnation and pantheistic spiritual evolution.<ref name="AHD"/><ref name="MW"/> Sometimes called Esoteric Buddhism.<ref name="OEtD"/><ref name="OED-online"/> {{defdate|from 1875}}
#*{{quote-magazine|last=Tuchman|first=Phyllis|authorlink=|title=Jackson Pollock: Modernism's shooting star|pages=|magazine={{w|Smithsonian (magazine)|Smithsonian}}|editor=|year=1998|month=Nov|volume=|location=Washington, DC|publisher=Smithsonian Institution|issn=0037-7333|url=|passage={{...}} his art teacher, who, besides nourishing {{w|Jackson Pollock|Pollock}}'s talent, would introduce him to '''theosophy''', the teachings of {{w|Jiddu Krishnamurti|Krishnamurti}} and {{w|extrasensory perception}}.}}
#*{{quote-journal|last=Coues|first=Elliott|authorlink=Elliott Coues|title=Theosophy: what it is not|page=133|journal=The Californian|location=San Francisco|volume=1|issue=3|month=Feb|year=1892|issn=2157-393X|url=http://books.google.com/books?id=G0xMAAAAMAAJ&pg=PA133|passage=That Blavatskyism and theosophism are identical is now a {{w|Argumentum ad populum|popular fallacy}} so deeply rooted as to be almost ineradicable. But the fact is, that the ingenious woman simply invented a scheme for exploiting herself, and called that scheme "'''theosophy'''."}}
#{{context|[[nonce]]-use|lang=en}} {{context|philosophy|religion|lang=en}} The philosophical speculation of {{w|Antonio Rosmini-Serbati}}, posthumously published in ''{{lang|it|Teosofia}}'' (''Theosophy''), about reasoning about God, in the [[signification]] that "God is the supreme Being and the apex of philosophical speculation."<ref name="Cleary2008"/> It differs from most theosophical systems, which start with a claim of intuitive or mystical insight into divine nature, or a claim of divine revelation, or a claim of spiritual ecstasy, while it, like most philosophy, starts with reasoning.<ref name="Cleary2008"/> {{defdate|from ''[[Appendix:Glossary#p.|p.]]'' 1855}}
#{{context|[[nonce]]-use|lang=en}} {{sense|in etymological sense}}  [[Knowledge]] or wisdom about [[divine]] things.<ref name="OED-online"/>
#*{{quote-book|last=Hamilton|first=William|authorlink=Sir William Hamilton, 9th Baronet|editors={{w|Henry Longueville Mansel}}; {{w|John Veitch (poet)|John Veitch}}|chapter=Appendix|title=Lectures on metaphysics and logic|page=416|year=1859|volume=1|location=Boston|publisher=Gould and Lincoln|oclc=|pageurl=http://books.google.com/books?id=S1tEAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA416|passage=An organ of Imagination is intimately connected with that of Theosophy or Veneration towards the vertex of the head.}}

